  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Monitoring potential for localized urban flooding \ntonight as a warm front lifts north.\n\nA stalled frontal boundary is currently located over the\nVirginia Tidewater region while extending back into central\nNorth Carolina. Any associated shower activity remains well\nsouth of the local area this morning. However, clouds do\ncontinue to lift up from southern Virginia, with further \nexpansion of cloud cover expected through the day. Looking at \ndry conditions for most of the area today as the boundary \nremains nearly stationary through the remainder of the morning.\nHowever, the boundary is going to start lifting north as a warm\nfront this afternoon, then make steadier progress through the \narea tonight. Showers and some thunderstorms push into parts of \nCentral Virginia this afternoon.\n\nA cutoff mid-level low over the Deep South today retrogrades north \nthis evening, and that is what will push the warm front into our \narea. As the front presses north, expect additional slow moving \nshower and thunderstorms to develop across central VA, then up \ntoward the Washington DC and Baltimore Metro areas overnight \ntonight into Wednesday morning. \n\nThere is a concerning signal in some of the high res guidance \nthat indicate potential for flash flooding in the metro areas \ntonight. Convection appears to be enhanced from the development \nof a broad wave of surface low pressure, or an MCV that develops\nin the aftermath of diurnal convection. Either way, any slow \nmoving shower or thunderstorm tonight is going to be cable of \nproducing heavy rainfall in a short period of time. Tropical \nmoisture returns as PWATs surge to over 2\" once again. This\nlikely yields high rain rates of 1-3\" per hour in the heaviest\nshowers/thunderstorms. A Flood Watch may be needed for parts of\nthe area, but confidence is too low at this time. Will need to \ncarefully analyze the 12Z and later guidance to determine if the\nthreat for urban flash flooding is there tonight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily shower and thunderstorm chances Wednesday \nthrough early next week.\n\nOngoing showers and thunderstorms continue into Wednesday morning as \nthe warm front remains somewhere north of I-66. The cut-off low over \nthe southeastern U.S. weakens/shears while subtropical ridging \nretrogrades toward the Atlantic coast. Increasing warmth and \nhumidity around the west side of the subtropical Atlantic ridge, \ncoupled with weak disturbances aloft will lead to afternoon and \nevening showers and thunderstorms each day Thursday into early next \nweek. Mid-level flow appears strongest Thursday through the weekend. \nGiven the degree of instability with temps in the 90s and 70s \ndewpoints, there could be a risk for severe thunderstorms each day. \nLocally heavy rainfall is going to be possible each day, as well. \nIncreasing warmth and humidity yield heat indices around 100 \nThursday through Sunday.",
